1
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:10,205
Imagine waking up tomorrow morning...

2
00:00:10,245 --> 00:00:14,532
and discovering that you are the only person left on Earth.

3
00:00:15,632 --> 00:00:19,491
No messages. No phone calls. No traffic.

4
00:00:20,591 --> 00:00:24,130
No voices coming from the apartment next door.

5
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:29,431
At first, you might think something unusual happened overnight.

6
00:00:29,471 --> 00:00:30,845
Maybe it's a holiday.

7
00:00:30,885 --> 00:00:32,797
Maybe there's a power outage.

8
00:00:32,837 --> 00:00:35,680
Maybe everyone is simply somewhere else.

9
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:38,878
But then... you look outside.

10
00:00:39,978 --> 00:00:43,443
And you realize that something is terribly wrong.

11
00:00:44,543 --> 00:00:46,843
Every street is empty.

12
00:00:47,943 --> 00:00:50,209
Every building is silent.

13
00:00:51,309 --> 00:00:56,013
And somewhere between yesterday and today... humanity disappeared.

14
00:00:57,113 --> 00:00:59,673
This is the story of Noah.

15
00:01:00,773 --> 00:01:02,656
And this is what could happen...

16
00:01:02,696 --> 00:01:05,010
if you were the last human on Earth.

17
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:16,721
Noah was twenty-seven years old.

18
00:01:16,761 --> 00:01:20,552
He lived on the ninth floor of an old apartment building,

19
00:01:20,592 --> 00:01:23,969
in the middle of a city that never really slept.

20
00:01:25,069 --> 00:01:30,129
Every morning began the same way. The rumble of buses. Construction drills.

21
00:01:30,169 --> 00:01:33,121
A neighbor's radio playing through the wall.

22
00:01:33,161 --> 00:01:37,051
Someone, somewhere, always arguing on the street below.

23
00:01:38,151 --> 00:01:42,727
Silence was something he had only ever heard in the middle of the night.

24
00:01:42,767 --> 00:01:44,969
And even then, never completely.

25
00:01:46,069 --> 00:01:48,640
But this morning... there was nothing.

26
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:50,549
Hello?

27
00:01:51,649 --> 00:01:56,361
His voice bounced between the buildings and came back to him, smaller.

28
00:01:56,401 --> 00:01:57,637
Nobody answered.

29
00:01:58,737 --> 00:02:01,781
He checked his phone again. No new messages.

30
00:02:01,821 --> 00:02:07,018
The last one was from his sister, sent at eleven forty the night before.

31
00:02:08,118 --> 00:02:11,271
Call me tomorrow, it said. I have news.

32
00:02:12,371 --> 00:02:15,394
His sister was three years older than him.

33
00:02:15,434 --> 00:02:20,498
She was the one who always called first. On birthdays. After bad days.

34
00:02:20,538 --> 00:02:24,334
After every storm, just to check that he was okay.

35
00:02:25,434 --> 00:02:29,280
He had meant to call her back last night. He had been too tired.

36
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:32,809
He had told himself there would be time in the morning.

37
00:02:33,909 --> 00:02:36,282
He called her. The phone rang once...

38
00:02:36,322 --> 00:02:38,625
and then the call simply dropped.

39
00:02:39,725 --> 00:02:41,501
Come on... pick up. Pick up.

40
00:02:42,601 --> 00:02:44,252
He opened social media.

41
00:02:44,292 --> 00:02:49,546
The last posts were all from the night before. Photos of dinners. Jokes.

42
00:02:49,586 --> 00:02:53,118
A video of someone's dog chasing its own tail.

43
00:02:54,218 --> 00:02:59,377
And then, at eight seventeen in the morning... nothing. Not a single new post.

44
00:02:59,417 --> 00:03:01,034
Anywhere in the world.

45
00:03:02,134 --> 00:03:05,269
At first, Noah laughed. A short, nervous laugh.

46
00:03:05,309 --> 00:03:09,241
The kind you make when something doesn't make sense yet.

47
00:03:10,341 --> 00:03:12,129
Maybe the network was down.

48
00:03:12,169 --> 00:03:15,109
Maybe there had been some kind of emergency,

49
00:03:15,149 --> 00:03:18,482
and everyone had been evacuated while he slept.

50
00:03:19,582 --> 00:03:23,525
So he got dressed, grabbed his green jacket and his backpack,

51
00:03:23,565 --> 00:03:26,261
and walked down nine flights of stairs.

52
00:03:27,361 --> 00:03:32,421
When he pushed open the front door of the building, the street was waiting for him.

53
00:03:33,521 --> 00:03:38,381
Cars stood in the middle of the road, some with their doors still open.

54
00:03:38,421 --> 00:03:41,279
A bicycle lay on its side on the sidewalk.

55
00:03:41,319 --> 00:03:44,575
A coffee cup sat on a bench, still half full.

56
00:03:45,675 --> 00:03:50,690
It looked as if everyone had vanished in the middle of their lives. Mid-step.

57
00:03:50,730 --> 00:03:51,734
Mid-sentence.

58
00:03:52,834 --> 00:03:54,197
Where is everybody?

59
00:03:59,297 --> 00:04:04,275
Noah spent the next several hours searching. For someone. For anyone.

60
00:04:05,375 --> 00:04:07,230
He walked down the main avenue,

61
00:04:07,270 --> 00:04:12,248
where on any other morning thousands of people would have been rushing to work.

62
00:04:13,348 --> 00:04:18,244
The traffic lights still changed. Green. Yellow. Red. For no one.

63
00:04:19,344 --> 00:04:22,230
He went into the supermarket on the corner.

64
00:04:22,270 --> 00:04:27,250
The automatic doors slid open to greet him, as if nothing had happened.

65
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:32,113
Music was still playing quietly from the ceiling speakers.

66
00:04:32,153 --> 00:04:34,342
Fruit sat in neat, colorful piles.

67
00:04:34,382 --> 00:04:38,210
A shopping cart stood in the middle of an aisle, half full,

68
00:04:38,250 --> 00:04:41,023
waiting for someone to come back for it.

69
00:04:42,123 --> 00:04:45,128
Three blocks away, he visited the hospital.

70
00:04:45,168 --> 00:04:47,677
The corridors were bright and clean.

71
00:04:47,717 --> 00:04:50,770
Monitors beeped softly beside empty beds.

72
00:04:51,870 --> 00:04:54,020
Is anyone here? Anyone? I need...

73
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:56,268
I just need to talk to someone.

74
00:04:57,368 --> 00:04:59,324
He went down into the subway.

75
00:04:59,364 --> 00:05:03,659
A train was waiting at the platform with its doors wide open...

76
00:05:03,699 --> 00:05:05,294
and every seat empty.

77
00:05:06,394 --> 00:05:08,755
He even checked the police station.

78
00:05:08,795 --> 00:05:12,733
Phones were ringing on the desks. Computers were still on.

79
00:05:12,773 --> 00:05:15,941
A half-eaten sandwich sat beside a keyboard.

80
00:05:17,041 --> 00:05:20,803
On the way back, he passed a car stopped at a green light.

81
00:05:20,843 --> 00:05:22,704
The engine was still running.

82
00:05:22,744 --> 00:05:25,917
The radio was still on, playing to an empty seat.

83
00:05:25,957 --> 00:05:29,785
Noah reached in and turned it off. He couldn't explain why.

84
00:05:29,825 --> 00:05:33,187
It just felt wrong to leave it singing to no one.

85
00:05:34,287 --> 00:05:36,288
The world hadn't been destroyed.

86
00:05:36,328 --> 00:05:38,457
The buildings were still standing.

87
00:05:38,497 --> 00:05:40,626
The electricity was still working.

88
00:05:40,666 --> 00:05:43,878
The trains were still waiting at their stations.

89
00:05:44,978 --> 00:05:47,828
Everything looked exactly as it should.

90
00:05:48,928 --> 00:05:50,954
Except for one detail.

91
00:05:52,054 --> 00:05:54,074
There was nobody in it.

92
00:05:55,174 --> 00:06:00,348
As the sun went down, Noah walked home through streets that seemed to grow larger

93
00:06:00,388 --> 00:06:01,568
with every step.

94
00:06:02,668 --> 00:06:07,641
And for the first time in his life, he understood how loud silence can be.

95
00:06:16,241 --> 00:06:18,621
That night, Noah barely slept.

96
00:06:19,721 --> 00:06:21,602
He kept checking his phone.

97
00:06:21,642 --> 00:06:27,058
He refreshed the same pages. He called the same numbers. Again. And again.

98
00:06:28,158 --> 00:06:29,755
Nothing changed.

99
00:06:30,855 --> 00:06:34,093
And then something happened that he didn't expect.

100
00:06:34,133 --> 00:06:36,250
He started talking to himself.

101
00:06:37,350 --> 00:06:38,816
Okay. Okay, Noah. Think.

102
00:06:38,856 --> 00:06:42,959
There has to be an explanation. People don't just... disappear.

103
00:06:44,059 --> 00:06:46,323
Not because he was losing his mind.

104
00:06:46,363 --> 00:06:49,350
But because hearing a voice... even his own...

105
00:06:49,390 --> 00:06:52,436
made the apartment feel a little less empty.

106
00:06:53,536 --> 00:06:55,940
He turned on the television.

107
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:01,596
Every single channel showed the same thing. An emergency broadcast.

108
00:07:01,636 --> 00:07:06,313
A government announcement, interrupted halfway through a sentence.

109
00:07:09,913 --> 00:07:11,368
Then static.

110
00:07:12,468 --> 00:07:15,395
Noah stared at the screen for a long time.

111
00:07:16,495 --> 00:07:20,653
Outside, one by one, the streetlights flickered and went dark,

112
00:07:20,693 --> 00:07:22,739
across entire neighborhoods.

113
00:07:23,839 --> 00:07:29,648
From far away, his apartment must have looked like a single tiny window of light,

114
00:07:29,688 --> 00:07:31,571
in an ocean of darkness.

115
00:07:32,671 --> 00:07:37,669
And for the first time, Noah understood that this might not end tomorrow.

116
00:07:42,769 --> 00:07:44,536
Three days passed.

117
00:07:45,636 --> 00:07:46,940
Then five.

118
00:07:48,040 --> 00:07:49,438
Then seven.

119
00:07:50,538 --> 00:07:54,904
Noah began recording voice memos on his phone. A kind of diary.

120
00:07:54,944 --> 00:07:57,682
Proof, maybe, that he was still here.

121
00:07:58,782 --> 00:08:00,322
Day seven. Still nobody.

122
00:08:00,362 --> 00:08:05,788
I went to the library today. Didn't take anything. I just... sat there for a while.

123
00:08:05,828 --> 00:08:07,895
It felt good to be around books.

124
00:08:07,935 --> 00:08:11,377
They're the closest thing to voices I've got left.

125
00:08:12,477 --> 00:08:17,250
He understood that he couldn't spend the rest of his life waiting for someone to

126
00:08:17,290 --> 00:08:18,042
come back.

127
00:08:19,142 --> 00:08:20,569
The world had changed.

128
00:08:20,609 --> 00:08:22,692
And he had to change with it.

129
00:08:23,792 --> 00:08:27,098
He learned which stores still had bottled water.

130
00:08:27,138 --> 00:08:31,490
He collected batteries, flashlights, and a small camping stove.

131
00:08:31,530 --> 00:08:36,351
He learned to cook on a balcony, nine floors above an empty street.

132
00:08:37,451 --> 00:08:40,912
The city that once felt crowded now felt enormous.

133
00:08:40,952 --> 00:08:46,773
A simple walk to the end of the block became an experience of complete isolation.

134
00:08:47,873 --> 00:08:49,817
On the fourth day, it rained.

135
00:08:49,857 --> 00:08:55,276
He watched the water run across the windshields of a thousand abandoned cars.

136
00:08:56,376 --> 00:09:02,463
Without other people, even the most ordinary moments became strange. Eating alone.

137
00:09:03,563 --> 00:09:05,965
Watching the sunset alone.

138
00:09:07,065 --> 00:09:09,196
Celebrating... nothing.

139
00:09:10,296 --> 00:09:12,821
At night, the city was completely dark.

140
00:09:12,861 --> 00:09:18,149
And without its lights, something strange happened above it. The stars came back.

141
00:09:18,189 --> 00:09:19,333
Thousands of them.

142
00:09:19,373 --> 00:09:24,653
Noah had lived his whole life under that sky, and he had never really seen it.

143
00:09:25,753 --> 00:09:27,830
Day eight. There are so many stars.

144
00:09:27,870 --> 00:09:29,947
I didn't know there were this many.

145
00:09:29,987 --> 00:09:33,757
Mom used to say they're just people who left a light on for us.

146
00:09:33,797 --> 00:09:36,790
I never understood what she meant... until now.

147
00:09:37,890 --> 00:09:40,797
There was nobody to tell about a discovery.

148
00:09:40,837 --> 00:09:42,716
Nobody to share a joke with.

149
00:09:42,756 --> 00:09:46,127
Nobody to ask whether tomorrow would be better.

150
00:09:47,227 --> 00:09:47,727
Day nine.

151
00:09:47,767 --> 00:09:52,288
I caught myself saying good morning to the coffee machine today. So... yeah.

152
00:09:52,328 --> 00:09:53,678
That's where we are.

153
00:09:54,778 --> 00:09:58,704
And perhaps the hardest part wasn't being physically alone.

154
00:09:58,744 --> 00:10:03,128
It was having nobody left who remembered the same world he did.

155
00:10:08,228 --> 00:10:13,161
On the tenth day, Noah went looking for something he should have looked for at the

156
00:10:13,201 --> 00:10:14,807
very beginning. A radio.

157
00:10:15,907 --> 00:10:20,924
If anyone else had survived, they might be trying to reach others the old way.

158
00:10:20,964 --> 00:10:23,513
Without the internet. Without phones.

159
00:10:24,613 --> 00:10:28,839
The electronics store on Fifth Street was dark and dusty.

160
00:10:28,879 --> 00:10:33,743
Rows of televisions stared down from the walls like empty eyes.

161
00:10:34,843 --> 00:10:40,013
He found a battery-powered emergency radio, the kind people keep for storms.

162
00:10:40,053 --> 00:10:43,356
He turned the dial slowly, through the static.

163
00:10:47,456 --> 00:10:48,030
Nothing.

164
00:10:48,070 --> 00:10:51,600
Just white noise, frequency after frequency.

165
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:54,352
He was about to give up...

166
00:10:54,392 --> 00:10:57,014
when a small red light began to blink.

167
00:10:58,114 --> 00:10:58,875
...is anyone...

168
00:10:58,915 --> 00:11:00,720
receiving this transmission...?

169
00:11:01,820 --> 00:11:03,269
Noah froze.

170
00:11:04,369 --> 00:11:07,381
Hello? Hello! Yes! I can hear you! Can you hear me?

171
00:11:08,481 --> 00:11:11,299
...if anyone is listening... please... respond...

172
00:11:12,399 --> 00:11:18,521
For the first time in ten days, Noah heard another human voice. Weak. Distorted.

173
00:11:18,561 --> 00:11:20,483
But unmistakably human.

174
00:11:21,583 --> 00:11:26,881
He held the radio with shaking hands and pressed every button he could find.

175
00:11:26,921 --> 00:11:29,318
And then the signal disappeared.

176
00:11:30,418 --> 00:11:33,613
No, no, no. Come back. Please. Please come back.

177
00:11:37,313 --> 00:11:40,338
If you can hear me... go to the old observatory.

178
00:11:42,838 --> 00:11:43,961
Look at the sky.

179
00:11:45,061 --> 00:11:47,388
And the transmission ended.

180
00:11:48,488 --> 00:11:51,579
Noah stood completely still in the dark store.

181
00:11:51,619 --> 00:11:55,936
He had spent ten days believing he was the last person on Earth.

182
00:11:55,976 --> 00:11:59,053
And now, someone was telling him otherwise.

183
00:12:04,153 --> 00:12:07,398
The old observatory sat on top of Mount Hollis,

184
00:12:07,438 --> 00:12:10,194
more than sixty miles north of the city.

185
00:12:10,234 --> 00:12:15,487
Noah had been there once, on a school trip, when he was eleven years old.

186
00:12:16,587 --> 00:12:19,141
He didn't know who had sent the message.

187
00:12:19,181 --> 00:12:21,540
He didn't know whether it was real...

188
00:12:21,580 --> 00:12:24,842
or just a recording, looping for days to no one.

189
00:12:25,942 --> 00:12:27,299
But he knew one thing.

190
00:12:27,339 --> 00:12:31,680
He couldn't ignore the possibility that someone else had survived.

191
00:12:32,780 --> 00:12:37,727
He packed what he could carry. Water. Food. A flashlight. The radio.

192
00:12:37,767 --> 00:12:39,604
And he left at sunrise.

193
00:12:40,704 --> 00:12:45,931
As he walked, the city slowly fell away behind him. Towers became shapes.

194
00:12:45,971 --> 00:12:48,790
Shapes became shadows on the horizon.

195
00:12:49,890 --> 00:12:52,794
The highway out of town was full of cars,

196
00:12:52,834 --> 00:12:56,717
frozen in a traffic jam that would never move again.

197
00:12:57,817 --> 00:13:03,373
By the second day, the road had started to disappear beneath grass and wildflowers.

198
00:13:03,413 --> 00:13:06,058
Nature wasn't waiting for permission.

199
00:13:07,158 --> 00:13:12,266
The sounds of the city were replaced by wind, by rain... and by birds.

200
00:13:12,306 --> 00:13:13,485
So many birds.

201
00:13:14,585 --> 00:13:17,993
Day twelve. Walked maybe twenty miles. My feet hate me.

202
00:13:18,033 --> 00:13:21,567
But I saw deer on the highway this morning. Five of them.

203
00:13:21,607 --> 00:13:25,957
They just stood there and watched me... like I was the strange one.

204
00:13:27,057 --> 00:13:32,517
That night, he slept beside a small fire, under more stars than he had ever seen.

205
00:13:32,557 --> 00:13:36,577
Without the glow of the cities, the sky was overwhelming.

206
00:13:37,677 --> 00:13:43,360
For the first time, Noah saw the world without the constant noise of humanity.

207
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:47,688
And it was both beautiful... and terrifying.

208
00:13:48,788 --> 00:13:52,031
In the afternoon, a storm rolled in from the west.

209
00:13:52,071 --> 00:13:57,539
Noah sheltered under a highway bridge while the rain hammered the road for hours.

210
00:13:58,639 --> 00:14:00,394
What if there's nobody there?

211
00:14:00,434 --> 00:14:02,189
What if I walk sixty miles...

212
00:14:02,229 --> 00:14:07,021
and it's just a machine, playing the same message over and over? Then what?

213
00:14:08,121 --> 00:14:09,175
Nobody answered.

214
00:14:09,215 --> 00:14:13,741
But in the morning, the rain had stopped. And Noah kept walking.

215
00:14:14,841 --> 00:14:19,556
On the third day, the road began to climb. Pine trees. Cold air.

216
00:14:19,596 --> 00:14:22,867
Clouds drifting below him, in the valleys.

217
00:14:23,967 --> 00:14:25,882
The planet was still alive.

218
00:14:25,922 --> 00:14:28,244
Even if its cities were empty.

219
00:14:29,344 --> 00:14:32,062
And then, through the fog, he saw it.

220
00:14:40,662 --> 00:14:42,747
The observatory was enormous.

221
00:14:42,787 --> 00:14:48,654
A white dome against a grey sky, its metal panels streaked with years of rain.

222
00:14:49,754 --> 00:14:51,400
The front gate was open.

223
00:14:51,440 --> 00:14:56,506
And inside, behind one of the high windows, a small light was glowing.

224
00:14:57,606 --> 00:15:00,476
Hello? Is anyone here? I heard your message!

225
00:15:01,576 --> 00:15:02,308
No answer.

226
00:15:02,348 --> 00:15:06,588
Just the echo of his own voice, rising into the dome.

227
00:15:07,688 --> 00:15:13,343
Inside, he found computers, old star charts, and rows of screens. Coffee cups.

228
00:15:13,383 --> 00:15:15,314
Notes scribbled in a hurry.

229
00:15:15,354 --> 00:15:18,790
A coat, still hanging on the back of a chair.

230
00:15:19,890 --> 00:15:22,057
Most of the screens were dark.

231
00:15:22,097 --> 00:15:24,191
But one monitor was still on.

232
00:15:24,231 --> 00:15:26,883
On it, a single word was blinking.

233
00:15:27,983 --> 00:15:30,422
Recording. Noah pressed play.

234
00:15:31,522 --> 00:15:32,729
If you're watching this...

235
00:15:32,769 --> 00:15:34,310
then the transmission worked.

236
00:15:35,410 --> 00:15:38,953
A woman appeared on the screen. She looked tired. Pale.

237
00:15:38,993 --> 00:15:42,921
She was speaking quickly, as if she didn't have much time.

238
00:15:44,021 --> 00:15:45,719
My name is Doctor Elena Carter.

239
00:15:45,759 --> 00:15:48,691
I'm an astrophysicist here at the Hollis Observatory.

240
00:15:48,731 --> 00:15:51,517
I don't know if anyone else is still out there.

241
00:15:52,617 --> 00:15:56,560
She looked directly into the camera. Directly at him.

242
00:15:57,660 --> 00:16:00,540
We've been monitoring the disappearance for several days now.

243
00:16:00,580 --> 00:16:02,932
But there is something you need to understand.

244
00:16:04,032 --> 00:16:06,770
Noah leaned closer to the screen.

245
00:16:07,870 --> 00:16:09,857
The disappearance... wasn't random.

246
00:16:10,957 --> 00:16:12,029
The image froze.

247
00:16:12,069 --> 00:16:16,391
Pixels scattered across her face. And the screen went black.

248
00:16:23,691 --> 00:16:26,701
Noah searched the observatory for hours.

249
00:16:27,801 --> 00:16:31,790
He found dozens of recordings, carefully labeled by date.

250
00:16:31,830 --> 00:16:35,677
And photographs of the night sky, pinned to every wall,

251
00:16:35,717 --> 00:16:38,765
covered in circles and handwritten notes.

252
00:16:39,865 --> 00:16:42,859
One of the notes was underlined three times.

253
00:16:42,899 --> 00:16:47,324
It said: The pattern is not natural. Someone is counting down.

254
00:16:48,424 --> 00:16:51,982
Each recording documented the same strange event.

255
00:16:52,022 --> 00:16:55,065
At exactly eight seventeen in the morning,

256
00:16:55,105 --> 00:17:00,321
a massive electromagnetic disturbance swept across the entire planet.

257
00:17:01,421 --> 00:17:06,997
In less than a second, every human signal on Earth disappeared. Phones.

258
00:17:07,037 --> 00:17:11,380
Heart monitors. Car radios. Billions of voices. Gone.

259
00:17:12,480 --> 00:17:15,046
But something else caught his attention.

260
00:17:16,146 --> 00:17:19,599
The observatory had been receiving another signal.

261
00:17:19,639 --> 00:17:24,890
A faint, repeating pattern, picked up by the radio telescope on the roof.

262
00:17:25,990 --> 00:17:27,964
And according to the logs...

263
00:17:28,004 --> 00:17:32,542
that signal had started eleven days before humanity vanished.

264
00:17:33,642 --> 00:17:35,512
Eleven days before... They knew.

265
00:17:35,552 --> 00:17:37,553
They knew something was coming.

266
00:17:38,653 --> 00:17:40,679
He found one last recording.

267
00:17:40,719 --> 00:17:44,264
It was dated the morning of the disappearance.

268
00:17:45,364 --> 00:17:49,241
We thought we were searching for another civilization. Somewhere out there...

269
00:17:49,281 --> 00:17:50,245
among the stars.

270
00:17:51,345 --> 00:17:53,555
She paused for a long time.

271
00:17:54,655 --> 00:17:57,482
But the signal isn't coming from somewhere far away.

272
00:17:58,582 --> 00:18:00,109
She looked frightened.

273
00:18:01,209 --> 00:18:02,720
It's coming from Earth.

274
00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:06,060
Noah stepped back from the screen.

275
00:18:07,160 --> 00:18:11,333
Below her frozen face, a final line of text appeared.

276
00:18:12,433 --> 00:18:14,716
Signal source... unknown.

277
00:18:15,816 --> 00:18:17,282
And for the first time,

278
00:18:17,322 --> 00:18:21,929
Noah realized that the disappearance might not be the end of the story.

279
00:18:21,969 --> 00:18:25,458
It might be the beginning of something much bigger.

280
00:18:30,558 --> 00:18:32,715
Night fell over the mountain.

281
00:18:33,815 --> 00:18:38,286
Noah sat among the screens, trying to understand what he had seen.

282
00:18:38,326 --> 00:18:41,073
And then, the lights began to flicker.

283
00:18:42,173 --> 00:18:45,816
The radio in his backpack turned on by itself.

284
00:18:46,916 --> 00:18:47,739
Noah...

285
00:18:48,839 --> 00:18:50,037
He froze.

286
00:18:51,137 --> 00:18:52,171
Who said that?

287
00:18:53,271 --> 00:18:55,062
Noah... if you can hear this...

288
00:18:56,162 --> 00:18:57,612
The voice was familiar.

289
00:18:57,652 --> 00:19:01,105
He would have known it anywhere. It was his sister.

290
00:19:02,205 --> 00:19:06,808
The sister who always called first. The call he never returned.

291
00:19:07,908 --> 00:19:09,552
I'm sorry we couldn't reach you.

292
00:19:10,652 --> 00:19:13,486
Where are you? Are you okay? Are you... alive?

293
00:19:17,186 --> 00:19:18,268
Look outside.

294
00:19:19,368 --> 00:19:22,240
Noah turned toward the observatory window.

295
00:19:22,280 --> 00:19:25,065
The clouds were beginning to separate.

296
00:19:26,165 --> 00:19:28,085
You were never supposed to be alone.

297
00:19:29,185 --> 00:19:31,562
And the transmission cut out.

298
00:19:36,662 --> 00:19:39,599
Noah walked outside, into the cold.

299
00:19:40,699 --> 00:19:46,341
Above the mountains, a strange pattern of lights was appearing across the sky.

300
00:19:46,381 --> 00:19:47,579
Slowly. Silently.

301
00:19:47,619 --> 00:19:50,639
One by one, the lights moved into place...

302
00:19:50,679 --> 00:19:53,451
until they formed a gigantic circle.

303
00:19:54,551 --> 00:19:58,791
He didn't understand what he was seeing. But he couldn't look away.

304
00:19:59,891 --> 00:20:05,294
Then, behind him, every screen inside the observatory turned on at once.

305
00:20:06,394 --> 00:20:09,621
The same message appeared on all of them.

306
00:20:10,721 --> 00:20:13,647
Humanity. Temporarily disconnected.

307
00:20:14,747 --> 00:20:17,675
Then another line appeared beneath it.

308
00:20:18,775 --> 00:20:20,919
Restoration. Pending.

309
00:20:22,019 --> 00:20:24,918
Noah read the words again and again.

310
00:20:26,018 --> 00:20:29,691
And for the first time since that morning... he smiled.

311
00:20:30,791 --> 00:20:32,548
Maybe there was still hope.

312
00:20:32,588 --> 00:20:34,877
Maybe humanity wasn't gone forever.

313
00:20:34,917 --> 00:20:40,324
Maybe someone, somewhere, knew exactly what had happened... and how to undo it.

314
00:20:41,424 --> 00:20:44,345
But before he could understand the message...

315
00:20:44,385 --> 00:20:46,707
the entire observatory went dark.

316
00:20:49,807 --> 00:20:51,446
Phase two begins tomorrow.

317
00:21:02,846 --> 00:21:07,794
The sun rose over the mountains the next morning... the way it always had.

318
00:21:08,894 --> 00:21:12,163
Far below, the empty city caught the first light.

319
00:21:12,203 --> 00:21:15,730
And in its silent streets, the birds had returned.

320
00:21:16,830 --> 00:21:21,638
Imagine living in a world where every familiar voice has disappeared.

321
00:21:21,678 --> 00:21:26,535
Where every street tells the story of a life that suddenly stopped.

322
00:21:27,635 --> 00:21:31,418
Where the only thing you can do... is keep moving forward.

323
00:21:32,518 --> 00:21:35,100
Noah doesn't know what tomorrow will bring.

324
00:21:35,140 --> 00:21:37,295
He doesn't know who sent the signal.

325
00:21:37,335 --> 00:21:41,388
And he doesn't know whether the people he lost will ever return.

326
00:21:42,488 --> 00:21:44,755
But one thing has changed.

327
00:21:45,855 --> 00:21:51,408
He takes out the radio and sets it on a rock beside him, turned all the way up.

328
00:21:51,448 --> 00:21:53,934
Just in case someone calls first.

329
00:21:55,034 --> 00:21:59,252
He is no longer simply waiting for the world to become what it was.

330
00:21:59,292 --> 00:22:02,365
He is trying to understand what it has become.

331
00:22:03,465 --> 00:22:06,226
Because sometimes, the end of everything...

332
00:22:06,266 --> 00:22:09,934
is only the beginning of a completely different story.

333
00:22:16,034 --> 00:22:19,111
What would you do if you were the last human on Earth?

334
00:22:19,151 --> 00:22:21,246
Would you keep searching for someone?

335
00:22:21,286 --> 00:22:24,611
Or would you accept that the world had changed forever?

336
00:22:25,711 --> 00:22:30,454
Let us know in the comments. And remember... this is only the beginning.
